<Docs
	page="recipes/lua"
	title="Lua"
	lede="No built-in grammar: load tree-sitter-lua from its release, and bring a query for your driver."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Loading the grammar</h2>
		<p>
			squill doesn&rsquo;t compile Lua in, so the rule names the grammar by
			its release URL. The first run downloads it and records its SHA-256 in
			a <code>squill.lock</code> beside the config; commit that, and run CI
			with <code>squill fmt --locked</code>. A loaded grammar has no default
			query, so every rule brings one. See{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#grammars">bringing your own grammar</a> for how both work.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Long strings, <code>[[&hellip;]]</code> or{" "}
			<code>[==[&hellip;]==]</code>, on one line or several. squill knows
			nothing about a loaded grammar&rsquo;s strings until the query{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#properties">says what they allow</a>, so the
			queries below promise that a long string takes line breaks and no
			escapes: a backslash in one is just a backslash. A quoted string
			can&rsquo;t hold a raw newline, so it&rsquo;s never rewritten; write a
			query you want formatted as a long string.
		</p>
		<p>
			A query built with <code>..</code> isn&rsquo;t one string, and is never
			matched.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>lsqlite3</h2>
		<p>
			The query takes the first string argument of the database methods that
			run SQL, with or without parentheses. lsqlite3 is SQLite, so the rule
			sets the dialect:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="lua-lsqlite3" lang="lua" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>pgmoon</h2>
		<p>
			The same shape for <code>pg:query</code>, in the default Postgres
			dialect. LuaSQL&rsquo;s <code>conn:execute</code> works the same way
			with its method name in the list.
		</p>
		<Recipe name="lua-pgmoon" lang="lua" />
	</section>
</Docs>
